{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"constructors","title":"Constructors","group":"Classes & Objects","order":27,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A constructor initialises an object when it is created. C++ has default, parameterised, copy and move constructors, plus member initializer lists, delegation and <code>explicit</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Kinds of constructors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass Person {\npublic:\n    Person() : name_(\"anonymous\"), age_(0) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"default\\n\"; }\n    Person(std::string name, int age) : name_(std::move(name)), age_(age) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"param\\n\"; }\n    Person(const Person&amp; o) : name_(o.name_), age_(o.age_) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy\\n\"; }\n    Person(Person&amp;&amp; o) noexcept : name_(std::move(o.name_)), age_(o.age_) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"move\\n\"; }\n    void show() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; name_ &lt;&lt; \" (\" &lt;&lt; age_ &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\nprivate:\n    std::string name_;\n    int age_;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Person a;                   // default\n    Person b(\"Ada\", 36);        // parameterised\n    Person c = b;               // copy\n    Person d = std::move(b);    // move\n    a.show(); c.show(); d.show();\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>default\nparam\ncopy\nmove\nanonymous (0)\nAda (36)\nAda (36)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Member initializer lists</h3>\n<p>Members are initialised <em>before</em> the constructor body runs. Initialise them in the list — assigning in the body means default-construct first, then assign. <code>const</code> members, references and members without a default constructor <strong>must</strong> use the list.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nclass Config {\n    const int maxUsers_;          // const: must be initialised, can't be assigned\n    int&amp; counter_;                // reference: same\n    int timeout_ = 30;            // default member initializer (C++11)\npublic:\n    Config(int maxUsers, int&amp; counter) : maxUsers_(maxUsers), counter_(counter) { ++counter_; }\n    Config(int maxUsers, int&amp; counter, int timeout) : Config(maxUsers, counter) {   // delegating\n        timeout_ = timeout;\n    }\n    void show() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; maxUsers_ &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; timeout_ &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; counter_ &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n};\nint main() {\n    int created = 0;\n    Config a(10, created);\n    Config b(20, created, 5);\n    a.show();\n    b.show();\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 30 2\n20 5 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Members are initialised in the order they are <strong>declared in the class</strong>, not the order in the initializer list. <code>B(int v) : b_(v), a_(b_) {}</code> with <code>a_</code> declared first reads <code>b_</code> before it's initialised. (<code>-Wall</code> warns about the mismatch.)</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hand-stop\"></i><code>explicit</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Meters {\n    double v;\n    explicit Meters(double x) : v(x) {}      // no implicit double → Meters\n};\nstruct Label {\n    int id;\n    Label(int i) : id(i) {}                  // implicit conversion allowed\n};\nvoid run(Meters m) { std::cout &lt;&lt; m.v &lt;&lt; \"m\\n\"; }\nvoid print(Label l) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"#\" &lt;&lt; l.id &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\nint main() {\n    run(Meters(5));       // must be explicit\n    print(42);            // 42 silently becomes a Label — often surprising\n    // run(5.0);          // error: no implicit conversion\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5m\n#42</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Guideline</div><p>Mark single-argument constructors <code>explicit</code> unless the implicit conversion is genuinely natural (like <code>std::string</code> from <code>const char*</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-robot\"></i>Compiler-generated constructors</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Special member</th><th>Generated when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Default constructor <code>T()</code></td><td>You declare <strong>no</strong> constructors at all</td></tr><tr><td>Copy constructor / copy assignment</td><td>You don't declare a move operation (deprecated if you declare a destructor)</td></tr><tr><td>Move constructor / move assignment</td><td>You declare <strong>none</strong> of: copy ops, move ops, destructor</td></tr><tr><td>Destructor</td><td>You don't declare one</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Widget {\n    int size = 1;\n    Widget() = default;                  // bring back the default ctor alongside another one\n    explicit Widget(int s) : size(s) {}\n    Widget(const Widget&amp;) = delete;      // forbid copying\n};\nint main() {\n    Widget a;\n    Widget b(5);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.size &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.size &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // Widget c = b;   // error: call to deleted constructor\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-confused\"></i>The most vexing parse</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Timer { int ticks = 0; };\nint main() {\n    Timer t{};        // an object\n    // Timer u();     // declares a *function* u returning Timer — not an object!\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; t.ticks &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why initialise members in the initializer list rather than the constructor body?</summary><div><p>Because members are always initialised before the body runs. Assigning in the body means default-constructing then assigning (wasteful for strings/containers), and it's impossible for <code>const</code> members, references and types without a default constructor.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can a constructor be virtual?</summary><div><p>No. When a constructor runs, the object's dynamic type is being built, so there's nothing to dispatch on yet. For “virtual construction” use a factory function or a virtual <code>clone()</code> method.</p></div></details></section>"}